{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1548529-hazards-of-the-dark-ages","id":1548529,"title":"Hazards of the Dark Ages:","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-02-16T08:50:01-08:00","id":1548529,"title":"Hazards of the Dark Ages:","content":"Is it Highwaymen? No. The Plague? No. It's stepping in ****. That's right, Medieval-times, **** was everywhere: if gasoline was the smell of the 20th-century, then **** was the smell of the 13th-cnetury: Especially in the towns. And, especially if you're a vampire walking out at night; the town streets were most likely dirt, with horse-**** all over the place, pig-**** and, of course, human-**** as well. And the alleys: there were few alley-ways as in most walled towns, every space was needed for housing, but there can be exceptions, they all smelled of urine and ****. So, if you're a vampire, trotting-out at night, don't wear your favorite boots. They will get, um, messy. Oh, and the moat, in most towns, that's called, **** creek. <br /> <br /> Night's Watchmen: they can be a problem; all towns and cities have curfews and it's usually at dusk. The bigger the town, the more aggressive the watchmen are. Smaller towns, they'll know everyone that's suppose to be out at night and be more friendly. <br /> <br /> The greatest hazard is, other supernaturals; there's a population-explosion of vampires and probably werewolves and maybe mages too. I think we crunched the math and given mortal healing-rates, a town of 5,000 could easily support a vampire. <br /> <br /> Yeah, Highwaymen at night can be a problem, for Mages and Inquisitors, but, they don't travel at night and if highwaymen happen upon a vampire or werewolf at night, may God have mercy upon them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-16T16:21:10-08:00","id":1548540,"title":"","content":"It's worth remembering that how insufficient human waste management was varied wildly by location. Though even so, it's not that much of a &quot;hazard&quot; since everyone at the time was well aware of it. The upper classes would just ride horses or in carriages to keep their boots clean. Everyone else just had brushes by the door.<br /> <br /> In most small-to-medium sized settlements in Europe in the 13th century, human waste was not dumped into the streets or anything of the sort. It was too valuable. Without the room for keeping livestock in the immediate vicinity, human manure was very useful for maintaining gardens and the like. The &quot;pail&quot; system was very popular in most of Europe: instead of outhouses where you defecated into a hole, then eventually covered the hole with dirt and moved the outhouse around, outhouses became stationary and a &quot;pail&quot; (usually more like a small barrel, not some small bucket) was placed under the seat. Full pails would be temporarily sealed, collected, and then added to composting sites (along with other organic waste) outside of the center areas of town to produce high quality fertilizer. Similarly many places used septic tanks or cesspools to contain human waste to one place that could slowly return organic matter back to the soil.<br /> <br /> Only in the largest cities where building and population density got too high for things like pail-systems to keep up where there were serious sewage problems; basically once having plants becomes a luxury. But the 13th century had already seen most cities figure out the transit of waste without making walking around impossibly messy... it was the smell that lead to the invention of what we would now consider sewer systems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-17T10:23:47-08:00","id":1548554,"title":"","content":"Highwaymen, robbers, etc. will not be a problem at night. Nobody travels at night, so robbers sleep during the night and are out ready to jump people during the day, when they are on the road.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-20T17:52:41-08:00","id":1548640,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jackob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1548529-hazards-of-the-dark-ages?p=1548554#post1548554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Highwaymen, robbers, etc. will not be a problem at night. Nobody travels at night, so robbers sleep during the night and are out ready to jump people during the day, when they are on the road.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just realized that; what's worse is, at night, Highwaymen are drunk and stupid.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1548529-hazards-of-the-dark-ages?p=1548540#post1548540\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's worth remembering that how insufficient human waste management was varied wildly by location. Though even so, it's not that much of a &quot;hazard&quot; since everyone at the time was well aware of it. The upper classes would just ride horses or in carriages to keep their boots clean. Everyone else just had brushes by the door.<br /> <br /> In most small-to-medium sized settlements in Europe in the 13th century, human waste was not dumped into the streets or anything of the sort. It was too valuable. Without the room for keeping livestock in the immediate vicinity, human manure was very useful for maintaining gardens and the like. The &quot;pail&quot; system was very popular in most of Europe: instead of outhouses where you defecated into a hole, then eventually covered the hole with dirt and moved the outhouse around, outhouses became stationary and a &quot;pail&quot; (usually more like a small barrel, not some small bucket) was placed under the seat. Full pails would be temporarily sealed, collected, and then added to composting sites (along with other organic waste) outside of the center areas of town to produce high quality fertilizer. Similarly many places used septic tanks or cesspools to contain human waste to one place that could slowly return organic matter back to the soil.<br /> <br /> Only in the largest cities where building and population density got too high for things like pail-systems to keep up where there were serious sewage problems; basically once having plants becomes a luxury. But the 13th century had already seen most cities figure out the transit of waste without making walking around impossibly messy... it was the smell that lead to the invention of what we would now consider sewer systems.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends if there are any gong farmers about. But if you're a vamp., out at night you're going to step in something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}